We use the definition of a hyperholomorphic 
connection as one with $SU(2)$-invariant curvature. 
Then \ref{_hyperholo_Yang--Mills_Proposition_}
follows from the

\hfill



     \setcounter{lemma}{\value{Mycounter}}
     \refstepcounter{lemma}
     \stepcounter{Mycounter}
     {\bf {Lemma \thesection.\arabic{lemma}}:\ } \label{_Lambda_of_inva_forms_zero_Lemma_}
Let $\Theta\in \Lambda^2(M)$ be a $SU(2)$-invariant 
differential 2-form on $M$. Then
$\Lambda_L(\Theta)=0$ for each induced complex structure
$L$.\footnote{By $\Lambda_L$ we understand the Hodge operator 
$\Lambda$ associated with the K\"ahler complex structure $L$.}

This is
\cite{_OSS_}, Ch. II, Lemma 1.2.4.
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

In \cite{_Bando_Siu_}, \ref{_UY_for_shea_Theorem_} 
is proved for K\"ahler $M$ (\cite{_Bando_Siu_}, Theorem 3). 
It is easy to adapt this proof for 
Hermitian manifolds satisfying conditions of Li--{}Yau.
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

The map $\widetilde \deg$ can be expressed in the
following wey. We call an integer cohomology class 
$\alpha\in H^2(M, {{\mathbb Z}})$ {\bf indivisible}
if its largest divisor is 1, that is, there are no
integer classes $\alpha'$, and numbers $k\in {{\mathbb Z}}$, $k>1$, such that
$\alpha = k \alpha'$.

\hfill



     \setcounter{sublemma}{\value{Mycounter}}
     \refstepcounter{sublemma}
     \stepcounter{Mycounter}
     {\bf {Sublemma \thesection.\arabic{sublemma}}:\ }
 Let $\alpha \in H^2(M)$ be an 
 non-$SU(2)$-invariant cohomology class and
$\alpha= \alpha_{inv} + \alpha_+$ be a decomposition
associated with (\ref{_H^2_isoty_Equation_}).
Assume that $\alpha$ is indivisible.
Then 
\begin{equation}\label{_tilde_deg_Equation_} 
   \widetilde \deg (\alpha) = C\sqrt{((\alpha_+,\alpha_+)_{{\cal B}})}, 
\end{equation}
where $(\cdot,\cdot)_{{\cal B}}$ is the {}
Bogomolov-Beauville pairing
on $H^2(M)$ (\cite{_coho_announce_}; see also
\ref{_gene_all_SU(2)_Theorem_}),
and $C$ a constant depending on $\dim M$, $\operatorname{Vol} M$.

The first claim is an immediate
consequence of {} Calabi-{}Yau Theorem 
(\ref{_symplectic_=>_hyperkahler_Proposition_}). 
Now, \ref{_hyperka_etale_Theorem_}
follows from the {}
Bogomolov's theorem 
(\ref{_Bogomo_etale_Theorem_}) and dimension count.
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

By (\ref{_Psi^F_and_multi_Equation_}),
it suffices to prove \ref{_Psi^F_on_m^iF/m^i+1F_Lemma_}
for $i=0$. In other words, we have to show that
$\Psi^F_{I,J}$ acts as identity on $F_x /\frak m F_x$.
We reduced \ref{_Psi^F_on_m^iF/m^i+1F_Lemma_} to the following
claim.

\hfill



     \setcounter{claim}{\value{Mycounter}}
     \refstepcounter{claim}
     \stepcounter{Mycounter}
     {\bf {Claim \thesection.\arabic{claim}}:\ } \label{_Psi^F_identi_on_F/mF_Claim_}
In the above assumptions, 
the automorphism $\Psi^F_{I,J}$ 
acts as identity on $F_x /\frak m F_x$.

\hfill

It is easy to modify
the proof of the direct and inverse twistor transform
theorem from \cite{_NHYM_} to work in 
{} 
quaternionic-K\"ahler 
situation. \hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

It is obvious from construction that
$\rho_{{\mathbb C}}$ is compatible with the complex structure
on $\operatorname{Tw}(M)$. \hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

Clearly, the map $\deg_\omega:\; H^2(M) {\:\longrightarrow\:} {{\mathbb R}}$
is equal (up to a scalar multiplier) to 
the orthogonal projection onto the line ${{\mathbb R}}\cdot\omega$. 
Then, \ref{_omega_gene_otho_Claim_}
is equivalent to \ref{_gene_pola_Definition_}, (i).
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

The variety ${\cal M}_F^I$ is compact by
\ref{_space_semista_bu_compa_Theorem_}.
In \cite{_Verbitsky:Hyperholo_bundles_}, 
it was proven that the space ${\cal M}_F^I$ of stable
{} 
{} 
deformations of $F$ is a singular hyperk\"ahler 
variety (see also \cite{_NHYM_} for an explicit construction
of the twistor space of ${\cal M}_F^I$).
Then \ref{_space_sta_bu_compa_hyperka_Theorem_}
is a consequence of the Desingularization Theorem
for singular hyperk\"ahler varietiess (\ref{_desingu_Theorem_}).
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

Clearly, $F'$ satisfies assumptions
of \ref{_space_sta_bu_compa_hyperka_Theorem_},
and the moduli space of its stable deformations is compact.
{} 
{} 
Since $\Phi_\gamma$ induces a homeomorphism of moduli spaces
(\ref{_admi_twi_impli_Theorem_}),
the space of stable deformations of $F$ is also compact.
\hbox{\vrule width 4pt height 4pt depth 0pt}

